WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:02.930
Picture a team that has taken up a new trauma

2
00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.950
informed approach to working with the public, with real enthusiasm.

3
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.610
People are proud of the change. Now another big change

4
00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:12.030
is coming, and two of the most committed people on

5
00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.170
the team seem exhausted.

6
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.520
That is a good problem and a hard problem at

7
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.140
the same time. The team clearly believes in the work.

8
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.300
So what does the page mean by transformational leadership?

9
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.750
Transformational leadership connects daily work to a shared purpose, while

10
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.290
developing the people who carry it out. Researchers describe four

11
00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:35.210
parts. Living the values consistently. Linking routine work to a

12
00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.110
meaningful future. Encouraging people to question old assumptions without fear

13
00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:42.350
of blame. And treating each person as an individual, with

14
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.510
coaching and stretch opportunities that fit them.

15
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.940
That sounds close to charismatic leadership, which also inspires people.

16
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.920
What makes the two different from each other?

17
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.430
The center of gravity. Charismatic leadership centers on the leader.

18
00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.970
Transformational leadership centers on the shared mission and the growth

19
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.650
of the people doing the work. Rather than creating dependence

20
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.850
on one person, it deliberately builds other leaders, so the

21
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.310
organization gets stronger over time.

22
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.760
What would I notice day to day with a transformational

23
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.600
leader? I am curious how purpose shows up in an

24
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.640
ordinary week, not only in big announcements.

25
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:19.530
Their calendar and budget match what they say matters. They

26
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.370
regularly ask whether a process still serves people well, or

27
00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.110
whether it continues out of habit, and they reward honest

28
00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.050
critique. Updates connect to real human impact. They invest time

29
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.970
in mentoring and in building depth for the future. And

30
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.410
they set high expectations paired with real support.

31
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.380
Where does this style do its best work? It sounds

32
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.300
especially suited to moments of big change.

33
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.270
It is one of the most consistently supported styles for

34
00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.830
long term effectiveness. It helps during major shifts, such as

35
00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.950
moving from rule bound, siloed work to service that is

36
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.150
centered on the person. By combining clear purpose with individual

37
00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.130
coaching, it builds motivation that comes from within, so teams

38
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.990
solve problems even when no one is watching.

39
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.340
Which brings us back to those two exhausted colleagues. Where

40
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.560
does the page say this style tends to struggle?

41
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:12.530
Overextension. Leaders drawn to vision and constant improvement can overlook

42
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:17.630
the steady basics: realistic workloads, clear procedures, routine checks. When

43
00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:21.110
transformation becomes a permanent state of urgency, even deeply committed

44
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:24.030
people burn out. And in agencies that have lived through

45
00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.890
several rebrands that changed nothing, a new transformation can meet

46
00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:28.550
real cynicism.

47
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.600
Let's turn to fair service. The page calls transformational leadership

48
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.540
a strong platform for equity, but it also names one

49
00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.120
blind spot. What is it?

50
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.730
First the strengths. Because these leaders connect work to purpose,

51
00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.350
staff come to see accessibility and fair service as part

52
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:50.990
of doing excellent work, not as a checklist. Inviting people

53
00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:54.090
to question old habits opens the door to rethinking hiring

54
00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.950
practices, credential rules, inaccessible forms and meeting norms.

55
00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:02.990
And individual coaching, offered fairly, helps build a more diverse

56
00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:04.310
group of future leaders.

57
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:08.100
And the blind spot? I suspect it has something to

58
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.880
do with that phrase, we are all one team, which

59
00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.080
can sound wonderful and still hide something important.

60
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.950
Exactly. This style focuses on improving the organization, not automatically

61
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.810
on examining who holds power. A unifying vision can unintentionally

62
00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.690
paper over real differences in outcomes by race, disability or

63
00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.930
income. And individual attention can drift toward people who share

64
00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.370
the leader's background or communication style, widening gaps while the

65
00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.030
leader believes they are developing everyone.

66
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.700
So what should the leader in our opening story do

67
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.000
about the next change, with two committed people already worn

68
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:42.280
down?

69
00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.230
Talk openly about pace. Ask the team what they need

70
00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.890
to finish the current work well, and stage the new

71
00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.750
change so no one carries both at once. The purpose

72
00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.370
stays, and people are protected. Another inspiring vision on top

73
00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.090
of exhaustion would most likely cost the team its most

74
00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.150
committed members first.

75
00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.760
That is a generous and practical answer. What does the

76
00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.440
page suggest for leaders who want to use this style

77
00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.300
well over time?

78
00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:11.150
Ground the vision in real data that shows where outcomes

79
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.270
differ in hiring, promotion, retention and service. Track who receives

80
00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:19.590
stretch assignments, visibility and mentoring, so development is fair rather

81
00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.250
than accidental. And pace change sustainably, retiring old work before

82
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.270
adding new demands, especially for staff who carry heavy emotional

83
00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:26.630
work.

84
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.920
Here is a question to carry with you. Who on

85
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.380
your team is carrying the most change right now, and

86
00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:34.320
do they know you see it? Thank you for listening.
